{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1558 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9638b0af296348ffc88aac783c283f2049135d9a03632c5bb652153c0b845c84" :edb/db-before-t 1557 :edb/db-after-t 1558 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534870 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:38:42.227Z" 13194139534870 true] [17592186124228 1100 "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2$." 13194139534870 false] [17592186124228 1100 "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2.$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186124228 1145 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186124228 1145 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - 0 &= 4x^{2} + 8(y + 2)^{2} \\\\\nz - 0 &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1/4} + ((y + 2)^{2})/(1/8) \\\\\nz - 0 &= ((x - 0)^{2})/(1/4) + ((y - (-2))^{2})/(1/8)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0)$. Also, from the equation, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{x = 0; y =-2\n$$" 13194139534870 false] [17592186124229 1100 "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2$." 13194139534870 false] [17592186124229 1100 "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2.$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186124229 1145 "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + 1 &= ((x - 1)^{2})/(4) - (y - 2)^{2} \\\\\nz - (-1) &= ((x - 1)^{2})/(4) - (y - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1)$." 13194139534870 false] [17592186124229 1145 "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-1) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1).$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186124283 1100 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+5) ^2}{3} = z-1$\nand the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a parabola. Find the vertex of this parabola." 13194139534870 false] [17592186124283 1100 "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+5) ^2}{3} = z-1\n$$\n\nand the plane $x=2$  onto the $yz$-plane is a parabola. Find the vertex of this parabola." 13194139534870 true] [17592186124283 1145 "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $x=2$, we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\n\\frac{2^{2}}{4} - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\n1 - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\nz &= -\\frac{1}{3}(y + 5)^{2} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane, we get the equation of a parabola that has its vertex at $(-5,2)$." 13194139534870 false] [17592186124283 1145 "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $x=2,$ we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3}(y+5)^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane, we get the equation of a parabola that has its vertex at $(-5,2).$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186124422 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316241 13194139534870 true] [17592186124422 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316248 13194139534870 true] [17592186124422 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316255 13194139534870 true] [17592186124422 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316304 13194139534870 true] [17592186124423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316262 13194139534870 true] [17592186124423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316311 13194139534870 true] [17592186124423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316318 13194139534870 true] [17592186124423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316325 13194139534870 true] [17592186124423 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316332 13194139534870 true] [17592186124477 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316269 13194139534870 true] [17592186124477 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316276 13194139534870 true] [17592186124477 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316283 13194139534870 true] [17592186124477 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316290 13194139534870 true] [17592186124477 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316297 13194139534870 true] [17592186125134 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534870 true] [17592186125134 1100 "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z = ((x + 1)^{2})/(2) + ((y - 2)^{2})/(4)$. Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" 13194139534870 false] [17592186125134 1100 "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" 13194139534870 true] [17592186125134 1145 "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nz-0=\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(-1,2,0).$\n Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316241 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316242 13194139534870 true] [17592186316241 1089 "q-164265" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316241 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534870 true] [17592186316241 1100 "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $3z+6=3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}.$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316241 1113 #uuid "7859670a-81dd-5793-b67c-4dd38017890f" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316241 1145 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+6 & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ 3(z+2) & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z+2 & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-2) & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(3,-2,-2).$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316243 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316244 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316245 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316246 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316247 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1041 "selection" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316243 13194139534870 true] [17592186316242 1110 #uuid "019ac036-0abd-5183-852b-1353823ad195" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316243 1034 "(3,-2,-2)" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316243 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316243 1111 #uuid "d351a1ae-fbf5-526e-a74d-c5ebc4a1f3b5" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316244 1034 "(2,2,3)" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316244 1111 #uuid "a65c3cb5-94e9-5e06-990b-ad47868d5e38" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316245 1034 "(2,3,-2)" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316245 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316245 1111 #uuid "7a0e75c4-9f3c-5053-999a-fcc40e3e6976" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316246 1034 "(3,-2,-6)" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316246 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316246 1111 #uuid "531834aa-b0b0-57f2-8669-46d4ea92eb2b" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316247 1034 "(-2,-2,3)" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316247 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316247 1111 #uuid "e8dc0bc3-4d59-5a8e-b54f-5b33b8c3b820" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316248 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316249 13194139534870 true] [17592186316248 1089 "q-164269" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316248 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534870 true] [17592186316248 1100 "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316248 1113 #uuid "94c5e070-73bb-51c9-9483-9e3d8ed68932" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316248 1145 "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nx-0=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-(-1))^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $x$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,3,-1).$\n Since it is oriented along the $x$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316250 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316251 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316252 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316253 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316254 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1041 "selection" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316251 13194139534870 true] [17592186316249 1110 #uuid "35258bf4-5705-5f8f-a501-65d3b320c69d" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316250 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=3\\end{aligned}" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316250 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316250 1111 #uuid "c0a10620-ca8a-5fc1-89f1-875f2b5a6904" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316251 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316251 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316251 1111 #uuid "812c118e-f906-53b7-ad74-af72498ef29b" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316252 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=3\\end{aligned}" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316252 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316252 1111 #uuid "df136940-dcc7-5f3c-930f-7bfbe8ea4e89" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316253 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=1\\end{aligned}" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316253 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316253 1111 #uuid "41501dc7-9a78-513e-8167-fcbae528db4b" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316254 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316254 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534870 true] [17592186316254 1111 #uuid "3e9b6dca-c841-5df9-84b7-a0a8cd79bc71" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316255 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316256 13194139534870 true] [17592186316255 1089 "q-164270" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316255 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534870 true] [17592186316255 1100 "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $y+1=\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316255 1113 #uuid "a5bc5f3e-997e-5815-95fc-e6a4a5e517e6" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316255 1145 "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\ny-(-1)=\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $y$-axis that has its vertex at $(-4,-1,2).$\n Since it is oriented along the $y$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534870 true] [17592186316256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316257 13194139534870 true] [17592186316256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316258 13194139534870 true] [17592186316256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316259 13194139534870 true] [17592186316256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316260 13194139534870 tru